WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.940
JESSE HALL: Let's discuss
agents, not just any agents,

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.910
but real-time multimodal agents.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.180
Many agents being built
today are single modal.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.010
They're text agents.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:15.630
And with text agents, latency
is much more forgiving.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.590
Users are okay with a
spinner or a text shimmer

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.510
to let them know
something's going on.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:25.110
But with real-time
agents, latency is key.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.750
And so that's what we're
going to talk about today.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.480
Real-time, it is
built different.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.890
What is LiveKit?

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.310
We'll discuss the cascaded
pipeline and then how Azure

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.740
and LiveKit work together.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.240
So before we talk about LiveKit,
let's discuss some challenges

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.650
that you will face when
building a real-time multimodal

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.390
agentic experience.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.000
You see, models, they
are the easy part now.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.640
Everything around it
is the hard part.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.380
Voice is one modality,
and voice is real-time.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.060
And so we have to
take the user's voice

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.820
from their microphone, wherever
they are in the world,

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.090
send that to the models,
generate a reply,

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.610
and send that back to
the user's speakers,

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.040
all within a few
hundred milliseconds,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.090
if we want to consider it
real-time, over networks

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:15.980
that we don't control.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.120
And so we have to
think about all

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.490
of the things that
add to latency.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:23.130
So network conditions, echo
and noise cancellation.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.700
We need to detect when the
user is finished talking,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.020
so turn detection.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.690
We also need to allow
the user to interrupt,

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.210
but we don't want them
to interrupt for a cough

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:36.660
or a sneeze, or if they're
back-channeling by saying, hmm,

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.210
oh, yeah, we don't want
that to interrupt.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:43.440
So there are a lot of things to
think about and a lot of things

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.440
that go into creating
an excellent real-time

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.600
user experience.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.050
And all of this has to work
not just for a single user,

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:55.230
but hopefully our application
is very popular, and it needs

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.650
to scale to hundreds, tens,
or hundreds of thousands

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.510
of users concurrently.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:04.830
And so, again, these challenges,
they are not the responsibility

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.800
of the models, but they
are the responsibility

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.680
of the real-time
media infrastructure,

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.110
which many are not
even thinking about.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.580
And so this is exactly what
WebRTC was designed for,

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.660
but building infrastructure
around WebRTC is not trivial.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.500
And so this is where
LiveKit comes in.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.980
LiveKit is an open-source,
real-time media layer

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.010
that your agent and your
orchestration runs on.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:36.180
So not just voice, but also
video and data built on WebRTC.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.670
And so then we have
the agent's SDK.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.220
You can choose between
Python and TypeScript.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.550
And these two things allow
you to bring your agent,

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.340
the same agent, to any device.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.840
So phones, laptops, SIP and
telephony, embedded devices,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.800
cars, wearables,
robotics, and much more.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.380
And LiveKit is the
transport layer

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.430
that ChatGPT chose
for their voice mode.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.740
Now, here is the voice pipeline.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.400
So, again, the user
speech is where we start.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.680
And the first thing
we need to do is VAD,

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.390
voice activity detection.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.480
We need to detect when
the user starts speaking,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.820
because we don't want
to process silence.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.770
That would cost us
a lot of money.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.170
So when the user starts
speaking, then we send

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.500
that speech to our
speech-to-text model,

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.160
which then transcribes
the speech into text,

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.870
sends that text to the LLM.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.540
The LLM generates a
reply, again, in text,

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.210
which sends that to the
text-to-speech model,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.750
which then generates the voice
that goes back to the user.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.360
So that's the very
basic voice pipeline,

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.140
or also called a
cascaded pipeline.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.120
Here is a zoomed-out
view of this.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.700
So, again, we have our user,
wherever they are in the world,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.600
on whatever device, and they're
connecting through WebRTC.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.290
WebRTC, every modern
device already speaks it.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.330
It is an open browser standard.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.820
And so then that connects
to the LiveKit server,

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.740
which handles transport,

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.770
jitter-buffering,
codecs, SIP, fan-out.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:08.940
And it's self-hosted.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.040
Again, it's open
source, so self-hosted.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.550
Or you can use it on LiveKit
cloud, and we host it for you.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.770
That is the transport layer.

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:18.990
Next, we go up to the
application layer,

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:20.550
or the orchestration layer.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.130
And this is where the
agent's SDK comes in.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:24.480
This is where your code lives.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.190
This is where you connect
media to the model.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.190
And so this, again, is where
the voice pipeline fits in.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.920
This is where you pick which
models you want to use,

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:39.270
where your tools, your MCP
servers, your custom functions,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.540
your custom business logic,
all of that sits here.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.510
So the box below agent's
SDK is a zoom-in

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:49.350
of what is inside
the agent's SDK.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:54.510
And so the key takeaway here
is that WebRTC gets users

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:58.650
to your agent reliably, and the
agent's SDK gets your custom

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:02.850
business logic to
your users reliably.

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.370
LiveKit is actually
model agnostic.

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:09.060
We don't have any models, but
this is not an exhaustive list,

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.310
but this is some of the
more popular models

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.110
that you can use
through LiveKit.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.870
So again, you pick
which STT, which LLM,

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:18.870
which TTS models
you want to use.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.690
You can mix and match
these however you want.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:23.010
And it's very easy
to swap them out.

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:26.610
It's just one line of code
to change out a model.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:30.510
Now, this is also
where Azure fits in.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:34.170
So we can actually use Azure
in all three of these places.

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.730
You can use the Azure STT.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.790
You can use OpenAI through
Azure for the LLM,

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.400
and then the Azure TTS.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.890
Now, as you're building with
LiveKit, this is actually,

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:47.520
I think, in my opinion, the
most important slide here.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:53.610
Please use our LiveKit MCP docs
server or the skill to make sure

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.940
that you are using the
most up-to-date context.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.140
So we ship new features at
LiveKit almost every week,

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.850
and our docs are amazing.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:04.650
They're always up-to-date.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.240
But your agent
doesn't know this.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:10.410
And so your coding agent needs
the most complete context

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:12.750
to build accurately
with LiveKit.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.730
So I prefer the MCP server.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.170
It's amazing.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:17.070
Let's get into a demo.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:20.880
Let's see how we can build
one of these out using Azure

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.530
and go ahead and talk to it.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:28.230
So first thing in your
terminal, let's do LKAppCreate,

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:29.370
and we're going to
use a template,

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.740
the AgentStarterPython template.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:34.950
And then we're going to install
another template using the same

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:38.100
thing, but it's the ReactStarter
template for our front end.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:41.760
Now, in the Agent template,
you'll find the Agent.py file,

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.430
and this is the
entire single file

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:46.650
where your agent logic sits.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.600
So first thing is we are
importing some things

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.390
from LiveKit agents
and LiveKit plugins,

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.360
namely the Azure
plugin for LiveKit.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:57.450
A little bit below this,
we have our Assistant.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.560
So this is our Agent.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.570
You can name this
anything you'd like.

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:03.600
But what we're setting up in
here is our LLM initially.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.940
And so, again, we're
using Azure LLM,

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.220
and these are some
environment variables

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.960
from my Azure deployment.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:14.610
So I'm going to be using an
open AI model here from Azure.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.110
Then we have our Instructions.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:19.950
Of course, you're a friendly and
reliable voice assistant demoing

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:22.950
at Microsoft Build, and
I gave it some Microsoft

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:24.570
Build information.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.990
We can collapse that, and we'll
move down here to OnEnter.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:30.210
When the agent
enters our session,

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:31.920
we want to greet the user.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:33.810
So we're going to give it
some instructions there,

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:36.660
greet the user
warmly, welcome them

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.060
to Microsoft Build, et cetera.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:42.150
And then we define our
server as AgentServer.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:44.460
We're setting up our
Solero VAD here,

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.650
and then here is our session.

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:49.530
So we're going to name our
session, named it MyAgent.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.570
But this is so that
the front end knows

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:53.100
which agent to connect to.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:56.460
So the front end needs
that same identifier.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:58.140
Now, below this is where
we're actually setting

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.610
up our agent session.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.700
And so here's where we
define our STT, our TTS,

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.620
and other variables here.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:08.490
But again, we're using the
Azure STT and the Azure TTS,

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:11.340
and I'm defining the
voice right here.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:15.090
When we start our session with
the agent that we defined,

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:17.670
and then we await
the connection,

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:20.130
and then we go ahead
and run this file.

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:21.450
It's actually very, very simple

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.550
to get this agent
up and running.

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:26.610
Next, we just need to define
some environment variables.

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:28.290
So you get three
environment variables

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:30.300
from LiveKit Cloud
once you've signed up.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:31.650
Of course, completely free.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.610
You'll also need some Azure
environment variables here

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.730
for your STT and TTS models.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:42.270
And then OpenAI API key, of
course, as well from Azure.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.190
And then we just CD into Agent.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:48.120
We're going to UV Sync, and then
UV Run the Agent Pi download

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:48.960
files, which is going

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.550
to download our small Solero VAD
voice activity detection model.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:54.150
And then we're going to CD

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.580
into the front end,
PMPM install that.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.610
And then we're going to UV
Run the Agent in Dev mode,

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.740
and PMPM Dev on the front end.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:03.780
So I've customized my front
end just a little bit,

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:08.230
Microsoft Build theme here, and
we're going to start talking.

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:11.040
COMPUTER VOICE: Hi there.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:13.170
Welcome to Microsoft Build.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.810
I can help with questions
about Microsoft technologies

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:20.280
like Azure, Windows,
Microsoft 365, GitHub,

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:23.610
or the latest AI work
Microsoft is sharing here.

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:25.920
What would you like to explore?

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:26.220
JESSE HALL: Awesome.

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:29.980
Yeah. Tell me, what are the
dates for Microsoft Build?

